@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Comms.ViewModels

@*
    The design's automated-messages list: each rule with a description and an on/off
    switch.

    The switch is real — it sets the template's IsActive — but nothing in Molargo acts on
    it, and that is the single most dangerous gap on this screen. A practice that reads
    "Appointment reminders · On" stops phoning to confirm, and the cost arrives as empty
    chairs nobody can explain.

    So the banner is not a footnote. It is the first thing in the pane, it is accented, and
    the switch is labelled with what the setting means — "would send" — rather than with a
    state that implies activity.
*@

<div class="max-w-[860px] px-6 py-5">
    <h5 class="mt-0 mb-1">Automated messages</h5>
    <p class="mt-0 mb-3 text-xs text-ink/55">
        Each of these is a template with an occasion attached. Turning one on records that
        the practice wants it sent.
    </p>

    <div class="mb-4 border-2 border-accent bg-accent-100 p-3 text-sm text-accent-800"
         role="alert">
        <strong>Nothing is dispatched.</strong>
        There is no scheduler watching for these occasions and no gateway to send through —
        both are online work. A message marked <em>on</em> below is a setting, not an
        activity, so keep confirming appointments the way you do now.
    </div>

    @if (Vm.Automated.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
            No templates have an occasion set. Give a template a trigger — before an
            appointment, when a recall falls due — and it appears here.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="flex flex-col">
            @foreach (var row in Vm.Automated)
            {
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3.5 border-b border-divider py-3">
                    <div class="min-w-[240px] flex-1 text-[13px]">
                        <strong>@row.Name</strong>

                        @if (row.NeedsMarketingConsent)
                        {
                            @* Marketing needs opt-in even when it is automated, and the
                               audience for it is a fraction of the patient list. *@
                            <span class="tag tag-accent2 ml-1.5">Needs opt-in</span>
                        }

                        <div class="text-xs text-ink/55">
                            @(row.Description ?? CommsViewModel.PurposeLabel(row.Purpose))
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="text-xs text-ink/70">
                        @CommsViewModel.ChannelLabel(row.Channel) · @row.Timing
                    </div>

                    <button type="button" class="@CommsCss.Chip(row.IsActive)"
                            disabled="@Vm.IsBusy"
                            @onclick="() => Vm.ToggleTemplateActiveCommand.ExecuteAsync(row.TemplateId)">
                        @* "Would send" rather than "On". The distinction is the whole
                           point of the banner above, and a switch reading "On" undoes
                           it. *@
                        @(row.IsActive ? "Would send" : "Off")
                    </button>
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink/55">
            @Vm.ActiveAutomatedCount of @Vm.Automated.Count turned on. When a scheduler and
            a gateway exist, each send will log to the patient's communication record the
            way a hand-sent message does today — which is what makes an unnoticed bounce
            findable.
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public CommsViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;
}
